6. Security Requirement

Document the security team’s requirement exactly.

Requirement

Sensitive credentials must not be stored in plaintext.

Credentials must be encrypted using AES-256.

The symmetric encryption key must be maintained separately in a secured location and must be readable only by the authorized Java application/job/service account.

Then convert that into technical requirements.

6.1 Functional security requirements

1. Passwords shall not be stored in plaintext.
2. Passwords shall be encrypted using AES-256.
3. The AES key shall not be stored inside the routine JAR.
4. The AES key shall not be stored in the same properties file as the encrypted credential.
5. The AES key shall be maintained in an externally secured location.
6. Access to the key shall be restricted to the application/service account.
7. Decryption shall occur only at runtime when the credential is required.
8. Decrypted credentials shall not be written to logs.
9. Encryption keys shall support controlled rotation.
10. Existing business functionality shall remain unchanged.

